oblems and intelligently automate processes. Business process modeling became the foundation of new methods, for example those that supported data collection, data flow analysis, process flow diagrams and reporting facilities. Business process modeling techniques have been developed over the decades to support specific business needs.</td><td>The term &quot;business process modeling&quot; itself was coined in the 1960s in the field of systems engineering by S. Williams in his 1967 article &quot;Business Process Modelling Improves Administrative Control.&quot; His idea was that techniques for obtaining a better understanding of physical control systems could be used in a similar way for business processes. It was not until the 1990s that the term became popular.</td></tr></table>

